WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.080
Can one signal at the edge of the sheet decide where a border falls?

2
00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:11.170
A gradient across the sheet needs a source of position.

3
00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:15.733
Part of it comes from signalling centres at the edges of the sheet.

4
00:00:16.717 --> 00:00:21.437
The cortical hem, at the medial edge, releases Wnt and BMP proteins.

5
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.950
At the front is a source of a growth factor, FGF8.

6
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.720
Necessary: remove or block it, and look for the loss or shift of what it controls.

7
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:37.653
Sufficient: add it, or add more of it, and look for a structure induced, enlarged or

8
00:00:37.653 --> 00:00:38.653
moved.

9
00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:45.807
In 2001, Tomomi Fukuchi-Shimogori and Elizabeth Grove ran both kinds of test on FGF

10
00:00:45.807 --> 00:00:46.807
signalling.

11
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.050
They used electroporation.

12
00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:55.130
Brief electric pulses drive injected DNA into progenitors at a chosen spot.

13
00:00:56.133 --> 00:01:00.733
Their readout was the barrel field, one cluster of cells for each large whisker.

14
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:04.913
Picture FGF8 as a lamp at the front of a room.

15
00:01:05.993 --> 00:01:08.533
Cells judge how far forward they sit by how bright it is.

16
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:13.407
Turn the lamp up. Add extra FGF8 at the front.

17
00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:15.887
Which way should the barrel field move?

18
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:27.500
Toward the back. Cells read themselves as farther forward, so each border falls

19
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.500
farther back.

20
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.320
That is what they found.

21
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.460
Later work showed FGF8 can act directly on cells at a distance.

22
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.120
Cells also respond through the gradients it helps set.

23
00:01:40.083 --> 00:01:41.343
Now block the lamp.

24
00:01:42.263 --> 00:01:48.303
They used a soluble receptor fragment that binds FGF8, and probably related FGFs.

25
00:01:49.323 --> 00:01:50.323
Where does the field go?

26
00:01:56.617 --> 00:02:00.237
Toward the front. More FGF8 moves it back.

27
00:02:01.217 --> 00:02:03.217
Less FGF signalling moves it forward.

28
00:02:04.237 --> 00:02:06.697
Opposite shifts mark a signal allocating territory.

29
00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:11.970
One more test. Put a new lamp at the back, far from the first.

30
00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:13.530
What should happen?

31
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.760
Near the new lamp, cells read a high level, as if they sat near the front.

32
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.100
Part of the pattern repeats there.

33
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.680
They found a partial second barrel field, behind the normal one.

34
00:02:31.633 --> 00:02:33.173
How far does a border move?

35
00:02:34.013 --> 00:02:35.193
A simple model helps.

36
00:02:36.053 --> 00:02:37.413
Its numbers are illustrative.

37
00:02:38.433 --> 00:02:41.813
Let the level fall by the same fraction over each step back.

38
00:02:42.613 --> 00:02:45.633
The decay length is the step that leaves a little over a third.

39
00:02:46.717 --> 00:02:50.237
Double the lamp, keeping the decay length and thresholds fixed.

40
00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:54.017
Every border on the sheet moves back by the same distance.

41
00:02:54.857 --> 00:02:57.777
That distance is about seven tenths of a decay length.

42
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.060
Halve the lamp, and each border moves forward by the same amount.

43
00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.700
A border pushed past the edge is lost.

44
00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:08.490
The equal steps belong to the exponential shape.

45
00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:11.330
A straight-line ramp gives unequal steps.

46
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.560
What survives both shapes is the direction, and a graded response to dose.

47
00:03:20.583 --> 00:03:23.663
The takeaway: graded signals help place the areas.

48
00:03:24.543 --> 00:03:27.403
In our working model, change the dose and the borders slide.
